WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 183: Lyndon B. Johnson: Master of the Senate and the 'Johnson Treatment' (1937–1963)

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.520
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:06.720
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:10.560
[Rebecca] Today we step into the whirlwind world of Lyndon Baines Johnson.

4
00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:21.840
[Rebecca] Larger than life, endlessly fascinating and bursting with Texas gusto, LBJ was a master of political persuasion who could charm, bully, or cajole anyone into submission.

5
00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:32.400
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story of the Texas Hill Country Dynamo, the legendary Johnson treatment, and the art of raw political power.

6
00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:42.160
[Rebecca] Lyndon Baines Johnson was born in August 1908 in a modest, unpainted three-room farmhouse along the Peternails River in Stonewall, Texas.

7
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:52.240
[George] The Texas Hill Country was a harsh, unforgiving landscape of caliche soil, cedar scrub, and scorching droughts that broke the spirit of ordinary farmers.

8
00:00:52.240 --> 00:01:03.360
[George] His father, Sam Ely Johnson J.R., was a charismatic state representative whose disastrous financial speculations plunged the family into humiliating rural poverty.

9
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:14.560
[George] Lyndon worked as a shoeshine boy, picked cotton under a blazing sun, and developed an insatiable, ferocious hunger for success, terrified of the failure that had crushed his father.

10
00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:31.440
[George] In 1928, pausing his studies at Southwest Texas State Teachers' College, 20-year-old Lyndon took a job teaching fifth, sixth, and seventh grade at the segregated Wellhawson School in Cotala, Texas, an impoverished, sun-baked border town.

11
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:42.560
[George] There, looking into the gaunt faces of Mexican American children who arrived at school without breakfast and walked in cardboard shoes, Johnson was gripped by a burning moral epiphany.

12
00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:56.080
[George] He used his own meager salary to purchase athletic equipment, organize debate teams, and promised himself that if he ever possessed real power, he would use every ounce of it to lift those impoverished children out of the dust.

13
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:07.160
[George] Those grueling months teaching impoverished children in Katala left an indelible imprint on Johnson's soul, giving him an abiding personal mission that would later blossom into the great society.

14
00:02:07.400 --> 00:02:17.720
[George] Teaching in Katala gave Johnson an unshakable emotional core, ensuring that throughout his career, the plight of the downtrodden was never an abstract statistic.

15
00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:23.960
[George] Elected to the House of Representatives in 1937 as an ardent New Deal disciple of Franklin D.

16
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:35.160
[George] Roosevelt, Johnson spent eleven years mastering the levers of power in Washington, bringing electricity and dams to his beloved hill country through the lower Colorado River Authority.

17
00:02:35.160 --> 00:02:38.520
[George] But his real ambition was the United States Senate.

18
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:45.960
[George] In 1948, Johnson entered the Democratic primary against popular former Texas Governor Koch Stevenson.

19
00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.080
[George] The campaign was a vicious, bare knuckle brawl.

20
00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:58.760
[George] Johnson barnstormed across the vast state in a pioneering helicopter dubbed the Johnson City Windmill, landing in pasture clearings to shake thousands of hands.

21
00:02:58.760 --> 00:03:03.960
[George] On primary election night, Stevenson appeared to have won by a slim margin.

22
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:23.640
[George] But six days later, in the corrupt southern border precinct of Alice in Jim Wells County, an amended tally from the infamous Box 13 miraculously produced 202 additional votes, 200 of which were cast for Lyndon Johnson, with the names written in identical blue ink and alphabetical order.

23
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:30.120
[George] Johnson was declared the winner by a microscopic margin of 87 votes out of nearly one million cast.

24
00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:38.840
[George] While mocking critics christened him Landslide Linden, Johnson embraced the cynical moniker with a chuckle, heading to the Senate where he would make history.

25
00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:50.160
[George] The disputed miracle of Box 13 demonstrated Johnson's ruthless mastery of the bare knuckle world of Texas politics, where every election was a fight for raw political survival.

26
00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:59.600
[George] The Razerthin victory in 1948 proved that Johnson was a survivor who understood the brutal machinery of power better than anyone in his generation.

27
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:08.640
[George] Upon arriving in the Senate, Johnson unleashed a psychological weapon that became legendary in American political folklore: the Johnson treatment.

28
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:16.160
[George] Standing six feet four inches tall with long, ape-like arms, massive shovel hands, and dark, hooded eyes.

29
00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.040
[George] LBJ was a towering physical presence.

30
00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.720
[George] When he wanted a colleague's vote, he did not merely ask, he enveloped them entirely.

31
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:41.120
[George] Johnson would corner an unsuspecting senator against a marble wall in the Capitol corridor, lean in until his nose was mere inches from the victim's face, grab their lapels, drape his heavy arm across their shoulders, and deliver a relentless, hypnotic torrent of words.

32
00:04:41.120 --> 00:04:44.480
[George] The treatment was an astonishing emotional kaleidoscope.

33
00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:58.000
[George] In five breathless minutes, Johnson would cajole, flatter, threaten, beg, whisper state secrets, weep for the poor, mimic mutual enemies, and appeal to patriotism and self-interest simultaneously.

34
00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.720
[George] Washington columnists Stuart and Joseph also described it as an experience resembling a tropical hurricane.

35
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:08.240
[George] The treatment was an incredible, overwhelming onslaught.

36
00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.000
[George] Its velocity was terrifying, its scope bewildering.

37
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.440
[George] One felt as if swept along by a tidal wave.

38
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:19.680
[George] Few men on Earth possessed the stamina to resist the Johnson treatment.

39
00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:31.800
[George] The Johnson treatment was a masterclass in psychological domination, proving that legislation in Washington was not merely a matter of ideology, but an intimate test of human will and personal leverage.

40
00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:40.920
[George] The legendary treatment became the engine of legislative achievement, turning the United States Senate into a dynamic force for national progress.

41
00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:51.400
[George] In January 1955, at the age of 46, Johnson was elected Senate Majority Leader, the youngest man to lead a major party in Senate history.

42
00:05:51.400 --> 00:06:01.720
[George] At the time, the Senate was a sleepy, sclerotic bastion of seniority dominated by conservative Southern committee chairmen who routinely buried progressive legislation.

43
00:06:01.720 --> 00:06:04.920
[George] Johnson utterly revolutionized the institution.

44
00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:16.920
[George] Working 18-hour days, fueled by furious sips of fresca and dozens of cigarettes, Johnson knew every senator's weaknesses, debts, mistresses, bank accounts, and pet projects.

45
00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:25.880
[George] He eliminated the rigid seniority system on key committees, appointing young, dynamic newcomers like Hubert Humphrey to influential assignments.

46
00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:30.040
[George] His greatest legislative triumph occurred in 1957.

47
00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:41.240
[George] Recognizing that no Southern politician could ever win the presidency without proving he could protect civil rights, Johnson outmaneuvered his former mentor, Georgia Senator Richard Russell.

48
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:51.000
[George] And Brokenfield navigated the Civil Rights Act of 1957 through the Senate, the first federal civil rights legislation enacted since Reconstruction.

49
00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:59.720
[George] It was a masterpiece of legislative alchemy that established Johnson as the undisputed master of the Senate, as Senate Majority Leader.

50
00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:09.160
[George] Johnson demonstrated that an unyielding focus on legislative results could overcome even the most deeply entrenched regional divisions in American politics.

51
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:19.760
[George] The 1957 Civil Rights Act was Johnson's political masterpiece, breaking 70 years of legislative paralysis and opening the door to the civil rights revolution.

52
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:33.680
[George] Outside the corridors of Capitol Power, Lyndon Johnson was a colorful, larger-than-life Texas character who delighted in outrageous practical jokes and earth-shaking hospitality at the LBJ Ranch along the Peternails River.

53
00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:44.480
[George] His favorite prank involved his rare, German-made 1962 Amphicar, a small, two-door convertible capable of driving on roads and floating like a boat.

54
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:54.160
[George] Whenever a dignitary, cabinet secretary, or unsuspecting White House guest visited the ranch, Johnson would invite them for a drive in the convertible.

55
00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:02.480
[George] Cruising across the prairie, Johnson would suddenly steer the car down a steep hill toward the deep, murky waters of the Peternails River.

56
00:08:02.480 --> 00:08:08.080
[George] Stomping on the accelerator, Johnson would throw his hands in the air and yell in feign terror.

57
00:08:08.080 --> 00:08:09.680
[George] The brakes have failed.

58
00:08:09.680 --> 00:08:11.360
[George] The brakes have failed.

59
00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:12.640
[George] We're going in.

60
00:08:12.640 --> 00:08:13.840
[George] We're going in.

61
00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:26.560
[George] As horrified diplomats and journalists prepared to jump for their lives, the car would plunge into the river with a massive splash and gently float to the surface as Johnson burst into howling, knee-slapping laughter.

62
00:08:26.560 --> 00:08:32.160
[George] Engaging twin propellers and cruising calmly down the river like a Texas riverboat captain.

63
00:08:32.160 --> 00:08:43.920
[George] The famous Amphicar practical joke revealed the unvarnished, roguish humor of a Texas rancher who took immense delight in shocking the self-important dignitaries of the Washington diplomatic corps.

64
00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:53.680
[George] His outrageous ranch antics were an expression of pure Texas authenticity, disarming the stiffest dignitaries with irresistible warmth and frontier humor.

65
00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:58.560
[George] In 1960, after losing the Democratic presidential nomination to John F.

66
00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:07.800
[George] Kennedy at the Lowe's Angels Convention, Johnson stunned the political establishment by accepting Kennedy's offer to be his vice presidential running mate.

67
00:09:07.800 --> 00:09:12.760
[George] Liberal intellectuals and Kennedy's younger brother, campaign manager Robert F.

68
00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:17.400
[George] Kennedy, were horrified, having fought bitterly with Johnson for months.

69
00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.840
[George] But JFK was a cold, calculating realist.

70
00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:30.440
[George] He knew that without Lyndon Johnson on the ticket, the Democrats would lose Texas, Louisiana, and the Carolinas, handing the election to Richard Nixon.

71
00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:42.520
[George] Johnson crisscrossed the South by campaign train, weathering spit and insults from conservative audiences to hold Texas and the Deep South for the ticket, delivering Kennedy's razor thin victory.

72
00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:49.240
[George] Yet once in the vice presidency, the Colossus of Capitol Hill found himself humiliated and powerless.

73
00:09:49.240 --> 00:10:04.680
[George] Excluded from Kennedy's inner circle of elite Harvard advisers, who snickered at Johnson's crude Texas drawl and rustic manners, Johnson languished in ceremonial obscurity for nearly three agonizing years, mourning his lost legislative kingdom.

74
00:10:04.680 --> 00:10:16.440
[George] The bitter years in the vice presidential wilderness tested Johnson's patience to the limit, yet taught him the crucial discipline of silence and strategic observation under immense personal humiliation.

75
00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:26.120
[George] The quiet years as vice president forged in Johnson a deeper resilience, preparing him for the immense constitutional responsibility that awaited in Dallas.

76
00:10:26.120 --> 00:10:32.760
[George] On November 22, 1963, destiny struck with sudden, shattering brutality.

77
00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:43.800
[George] Riding two cars behind President Kennedy in the motorcade through Dallas, Johnson was thrown to the floorboard by Secret Service agent Rufus Youngblood as shots echoed through Dealy Plaza.

78
00:10:43.800 --> 00:10:54.880
[George] Rushed to Parkland Memorial Hospital, Johnson waited in a windowless cubicle until Secret Service Chief Kenneth O'Donnell walked in and uttered the solemn words He's gone.

79
00:10:54.880 --> 00:11:00.000
[George] Knowing that a nuclear armed world was watching America in its hour of vulnerability.

80
00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.520
[George] Johnson acted with magnificent, steady resolve.

81
00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:09.280
[George] Hurrying to Love Field, he boarded Air Force One and summoned Federal District Judge Sarah T.

82
00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:15.840
[George] Hughes at 2 38 that afternoon in the sweltering, crowded cabin of the presidential aircraft.

83
00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:21.920
[George] With Jacqueline Kennedy standing beside him, still wearing her blood-splattered strawberry pink Chanel suit.

84
00:11:21.920 --> 00:11:28.880
[George] Lyndon Baines Johnson placed his massive left hand on a small Catholic missile and swore the presidential oath.

85
00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:38.960
[George] As the jet climbed into the Texas sky toward Washington, the raw-boned boy from the Peternails prepared to take the reins of an orphaned republic and reshape it forever.

86
00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:50.400
[George] In taking the solemn oath aboard Air Force One in Dallas, Lyndon Johnson stepped into the breach with an iron constitutional resolve that steadied a weeping nation in its darkest hour of peril.

87
00:11:50.400 --> 00:12:00.720
[George] In that dark, sweltering airplane cabin, Johnson provided the steady hand that held the Republic together, transforming grief into unshakable purpose.

88
00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:02.080
[Rebecca] Lyndon B.

89
00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:12.560
[Rebecca] Johnson took the presidential oath amidst tragedy, but he transformed national grief into the greatest avalanche of progressive legislation in American history.

90
00:12:12.560 --> 00:12:14.720
[Rebecca] Next week, we conclude our Lyndon B.

91
00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:16.000
[Rebecca] Johnson saga.

92
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:22.240
[Rebecca] The passage of the Landmark Civil Rights Act of 1964 and Voting Rights Act of 1965.

93
00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:33.080
[Rebecca] The creation of Medicare and the Great Society, his beloved White House Beagles Him and Her, the tragic quagmire of the Vietnam War, and the shocking broadcast when he walked away from power.

94
00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:40.840
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

